# .dockerignore — what NOT to send to the Docker daemon.
#
# THE SECURITY REASON THIS FILE IS MANDATORY, not merely nice: my Dockerfile
# does `COPY . .`, and without this file that copies .env — every API key I
# have — into a build layer. Layers are immutable and travel with the image, so
# deleting the file in a later RUN does NOT remove it; anyone who can pull the
# image can extract it with `docker history` or by unpacking the layer tarball.
#
# .gitignore does not help here. Docker has never read it, and the two lists
# genuinely differ: git ignores build output I don't want versioned, while this
# ignores things that are either secret or would just bloat the build context.
#
# The performance reason is real too: everything not listed here gets tarred and
# shipped to the daemon on every build. node_modules alone is hundreds of MB
# that the Go build has no use for.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- SECRETS
# First, and non-negotiable.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ------------------------------------------------------- version control / CI
.git
.gitignore
.github
.golangci.yml

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ frontend
# The Next.js app deploys to Vercel, not into this image.
web/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- python
# The solver has its own Dockerfile and its own build context.
solver/
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/*.egg-info

# ----------------------------------------------------------- local dev cruft
docker-compose.yml
Makefile
README.md
docs/
LICENSE
*.md

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Agent tooling — not part of the application.
.claude/
.impeccable/
skills-lock.json
